WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.790 --> 00:00:12.440
- So I'm really delighted to
introduce this event today.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:16.830
It's part of a kind of
subseries that we have going on

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:19.360
at the Council of Southeast
Asian Studies here at Yale.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.130
I'm the chair of the Council
of Southeast Asian Studies.

00:00:22.130 --> 00:00:25.370
And we have a graduate student conference

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.610
that's going on at the end of the semester

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:29.040
on the theme of waters.

00:00:29.040 --> 00:00:32.050
And it's organized by
a group of inter-Asia

00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:33.560
graduate students.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:37.030
And so Al Lim, who is here, and I

00:00:37.030 --> 00:00:39.840
got talking about the idea of water,

00:00:39.840 --> 00:00:41.940
in Southeast Asia in particular.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:45.220
So we decided that we
would have a side current,

00:00:45.220 --> 00:00:48.723
so to speak, leading up to that
graduate student conference.

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:52.660
Al, if you don't mind finding
the URL to the conference,

00:00:52.660 --> 00:00:53.730
you might put it in the chat.

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:55.733
I forgot to dig it up before now.

00:00:56.610 --> 00:00:59.770
But that will be taking place
at the end of the semester.

00:00:59.770 --> 00:01:02.280
And so then, we have these evening events,

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.210
which are in addition to our
council Brown Bag series.

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:08.870
But these events are all circulating

00:01:08.870 --> 00:01:11.770
on this theme of waters.

00:01:11.770 --> 00:01:16.180
So I'm delighted today to
present or to introduce

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.750
my friend and colleague, Andrew Johnson,

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:24.380
who will be talking about his
new book, Mekong Dreaming.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.050
And rather than give an
extended introduction,

00:01:28.050 --> 00:01:31.440
Andrew has taught at Yale
and US, at Princeton.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.910
I've known Andrew since
graduate studies at Cornell.

00:01:34.910 --> 00:01:38.783
I think his PhD's from around 2010.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.560
But rather than the standard introduction,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:45.390
I wanna just read a single
sentence from this book

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:47.360
to give you a sense of it.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:51.080
You're gonna hear more about
the full topic of the book.

00:01:51.080 --> 00:01:53.280
But I just wanna set the
tone for this evening

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:54.493
with this sentence:

00:01:56.387 --> 00:01:59.830
"How to think about such moments,

00:01:59.830 --> 00:02:04.127
when a glimpse into a higher
plane reveals a garbled,

00:02:04.127 --> 00:02:06.397
incomprehensible message."

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.153
Let me just read that
sentence one more time.

00:02:11.007 --> 00:02:14.210
"How to think about such moments

00:02:14.210 --> 00:02:17.160
when a glimpse into a higher plane

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.297
reveals a garbled,
incomprehensible message."

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:25.520
It's a really profound passage in a book

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:29.045
that's full of profound and
very interesting reflections

00:02:29.045 --> 00:02:31.700
on the art of field work,

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:35.930
about the encounters with the
supernatural, the uncanny,

00:02:35.930 --> 00:02:40.930
the inexplicable, and a
meditation along the banks

00:02:41.420 --> 00:02:44.240
of the Mekong, which is
really quite a fascinating way

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.380
to get us into our discussions

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.770
about waters in Southeast Asia.

00:02:49.770 --> 00:02:52.610
And to take us, last week we had a talk

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:56.350
in the Brown Bag series by
Jim Scott about the Irrawaddy.

00:02:56.350 --> 00:03:01.050
And so, now we've moved to
another important deltaic system

00:03:01.050 --> 00:03:03.610
in a important river in Southeast Asia.

00:03:03.610 --> 00:03:06.150
But here, I think what's
fascinating is we're taking it

00:03:06.150 --> 00:03:09.900
from the realm of the
material, and the social,

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.560
to the realm of the uncanny,
and how those intersect

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:15.450
with the material and the social.

00:03:15.450 --> 00:03:16.510
So I won't say any more,

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.330
but I wanna turn it over

00:03:18.330 --> 00:03:20.750
to my friend and colleague Andrew Johnson,

00:03:20.750 --> 00:03:24.540
to take us on a little journey
with today's proceeding.

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:27.710
So, I'm gonna mute myself
and turn it over to Andrew,

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:32.400
and he'll have a talk
for standard talk length

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:33.890
discussion of the book.

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.380
And then, after that we'll have time

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.050
for question and answer period,

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:40.230
and I'll moderate that
when we get to that.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:42.503
So, thank you Andrew, and welcome.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:44.293
- Thanks.

00:03:44.293 --> 00:03:47.100
Thanks so much, Al, and thanks
Erik, for that introduction.

00:03:47.100 --> 00:03:50.443
And thanks for inviting me here.

00:03:51.553 --> 00:03:55.210
It's a pleasure to be speaking
to (indistinct) at Yale.

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.890
And I'm really thrilled to be here.

00:03:59.890 --> 00:04:02.637
I'd also like to
introduce a friend of mine

00:04:02.637 --> 00:04:07.637
(indistinct) who is there
at the banks of the Mekong,

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:12.530
and he's gonna show some
video of the river where

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:16.190
what I'm gonna be talking
about, and some of the ...

00:04:16.190 --> 00:04:18.275
So you can kind of see in real time

00:04:18.275 --> 00:04:23.275
what's going on there as I talk about it.

00:04:24.281 --> 00:04:28.448
(Speaking in a foreign language).

00:04:40.863 --> 00:04:44.280
Okay, so without further ado, I'll start.

00:04:48.510 --> 00:04:50.750
This year, the Mekong river changed color.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:53.960
Its flow was normally a muddy
brown, red and local parlance.

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:55.960
But this year, as last year,

00:04:55.960 --> 00:04:57.900
the flow turned a beautiful blue,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.920
it's deep silty water became clear,

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:00.990
and those living out of it's banks,

00:05:00.990 --> 00:05:04.003
could see further down
than they could remember.

00:05:05.060 --> 00:05:07.120
But no one understood
the change in the rivers

00:05:07.120 --> 00:05:08.850
quality is a good thing.

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:11.810
Fishermen could see now
fish rotting on the bottom,

00:05:11.810 --> 00:05:14.577
and nets that normally
hung in slow moving eddies,

00:05:14.577 --> 00:05:17.408
and the flow became clogged with algae,

00:05:17.408 --> 00:05:19.800
(speaks in foreign language).

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:21.740
This green mass could be sometimes

00:05:21.740 --> 00:05:23.540
be pounded and roasted into a sort of

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:25.640
a Laotian version of Nori.

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:28.920
But whenever advantage this
newly abundant food source had

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:31.300
was offset by this damage to fishing gear,

00:05:31.300 --> 00:05:34.240
nets and boat propellers
became clogged and useless,

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:36.950
as thick green mats float
through the river and choked

00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:37.833
off the flow.

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.920
Further, even for unclogged nets,

00:05:40.920 --> 00:05:44.120
this clear water presented
another kind of a problem.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:46.080
The kinds of nets the fishermen used,

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.360
these thin filaments of blue polyester,

00:05:48.360 --> 00:05:52.040
rely on being invisible to
fish in red silty water,

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.943
hence the name (indistinct),a
trick of the eye.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:59.123
These tricks however,
don't work in clear flow.

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.250
In recent years, all in
the Mekong are dealing

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.960
with a radically different
river, with different water,

00:06:04.960 --> 00:06:07.320
a different biota, and
different techniques,

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:09.410
that one might use to deal with it.

00:06:09.410 --> 00:06:11.170
But this is not limited
to the area that I'm gonna

00:06:11.170 --> 00:06:12.100
be talking about today,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:15.517
the middle Mekong in between
Thailand and Laos elsewhere.

00:06:15.517 --> 00:06:18.310
One of the most significant
hydrological events

00:06:18.310 --> 00:06:20.720
in the world simply failed to manifest.

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:23.440
As the annual backflow
of the Mekong ceased.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:24.830
Now, normally what would happen,

00:06:24.830 --> 00:06:28.360
is as the monsoons flow into the river,

00:06:28.360 --> 00:06:30.270
the river turns back on itself

00:06:30.270 --> 00:06:32.880
flowing upstream into
the (indistinct) lake,

00:06:32.880 --> 00:06:36.070
and filling the lake in
the middle of Cambodia,

00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:39.057
which provides most of the
protein and food resources

00:06:39.057 --> 00:06:40.023
for that country.

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:42.330
But this year it didn't.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.810
For a fisherman this is
exceptionally troubling.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.130
The Mekong provides most of
the protein for Cambodia,

00:06:48.130 --> 00:06:51.080
and it's the heart of
Laos and of the Isan,

00:06:51.080 --> 00:06:53.210
the Laos speaking region in Thailand.

00:06:53.210 --> 00:06:55.220
And fisheries have
crashed in recent years,

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:57.163
and with them entire ways of life.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:01.150
The reasons for all of
these changes have to do

00:07:01.150 --> 00:07:04.410
with a complicated mixture
of hydrology and geopolitics.

00:07:04.410 --> 00:07:06.700
We could think about it in this way,

00:07:06.700 --> 00:07:07.940
if you want it to.

00:07:07.940 --> 00:07:09.840
We could think about national priorities,

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:11.090
or economic agendas.

00:07:11.090 --> 00:07:13.410
We could think about the
Mekong river commission,

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:15.920
as sort of multi body
international organization

00:07:15.920 --> 00:07:19.130
of Mekong States, that
seeks to manage and profit

00:07:19.130 --> 00:07:20.283
from the rivers flow.

00:07:21.220 --> 00:07:23.570
We can think about it in
terms of national developments

00:07:23.570 --> 00:07:26.460
of natural resources shared
between Thailand and Laos.

00:07:26.460 --> 00:07:30.350
But resources that belong
to this or to that nation.

00:07:30.350 --> 00:07:33.060
We could think about it as a
potential political flashpoint

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:35.270
between China and Isan.

00:07:35.270 --> 00:07:37.680
But, that's not really what I do.

00:07:37.680 --> 00:07:40.940
I'm an anthropologist, and
that means I sort of zoom in

00:07:40.940 --> 00:07:42.400
to ask what these changes mean,

00:07:42.400 --> 00:07:44.480
in the lives of ordinary people?

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.860
And what I wanna argue here,
is that the crisis in the river

00:07:46.860 --> 00:07:49.190
affects people on
multiple different levels.

00:07:49.190 --> 00:07:52.750
It's a crisis of ecology,
of hydrology, of economy,

00:07:52.750 --> 00:07:55.893
of family, of imagination, of religion.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:02.150
So, here if one weakness
that I think a lot of work

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:04.510
on water and hydrology has,

00:08:04.510 --> 00:08:06.940
is that it gets really open to metaphors.

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:08.710
And so the metaphor I'm gonna use,

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:11.520
is not quite flows, and not
quite streams of consciousness

00:08:11.520 --> 00:08:13.163
as this is entitled but a net.

00:08:14.050 --> 00:08:17.140
So relations are revealed
to be interlinked.

00:08:17.140 --> 00:08:20.240
And a mirror of the
blue (indistinct) nets,

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.270
new ecological, social and
spiritual realms are interwoven.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:26.700
It's a core contention here
that meaningful distinctions

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:31.263
between the ecological, the
economic, the spiritual,

00:08:33.496 --> 00:08:36.780
the social, are not really so evident.

00:08:36.780 --> 00:08:38.610
You might use the term ontological.

00:08:38.610 --> 00:08:40.400
Other people have used
the term ontological

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:43.450
to refer to a realm that
encompasses all that exists

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:46.260
within the world, but there's an important

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:47.543
caveat to that idea.

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:51.530
While all the ontological
configurations are in flux.

00:08:51.530 --> 00:08:54.780
There's no point at which
there's a harmonious status.

00:08:54.780 --> 00:08:56.920
There's one ontology that one could equate

00:08:56.920 --> 00:08:58.680
with a kind of a culture or tradition.

00:08:58.680 --> 00:09:02.060
Something that lasts unchanged over time.

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:04.520
Such configurations are always changing.

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:06.240
In a moment of ecological collapse,

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:08.270
relations are even more in flux,

00:09:08.270 --> 00:09:10.583
with catastrophic
effects and all involved.

00:09:11.540 --> 00:09:13.170
Further just as we have a net,

00:09:13.170 --> 00:09:17.040
that its strands extend out
into the Mekong's murky flow.

00:09:17.040 --> 00:09:19.920
The strands that make up
a net often lead beyond

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:22.170
the zones of apprehension.

00:09:22.170 --> 00:09:25.463
A bit like that passage that
Erik read from the book.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:28.850
We don't quite know where
these strands lead yet.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:31.037
But still we hope to follow them,

00:09:31.037 --> 00:09:32.663
and to harness their potential.

00:09:34.830 --> 00:09:38.060
Tim Ingold has talked
about these relations

00:09:38.060 --> 00:09:40.080
as a politics of dwelling.

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:42.722
It's a way to rethink a binary between

00:09:42.722 --> 00:09:46.543
a static agentive people and
a reactive passive nature.

00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:51.090
And to kind of break apart
the assumption of fact

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:52.680
that the idea of nature has.

00:09:52.680 --> 00:09:55.810
Nature in contrast, is
not the raw conditions

00:09:55.810 --> 00:09:59.070
out there to be perceived,
apprehended and utilized

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:00.680
by an agentive person.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.510
But which we change, and we are changed by

00:10:03.510 --> 00:10:06.130
this physical being with
the material and biota

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:07.630
with which we interact.

00:10:07.630 --> 00:10:10.150
Such a process of dwelling
creates what Ingold calls

00:10:10.150 --> 00:10:11.223
a sense of place.

00:10:12.700 --> 00:10:16.933
But here today, something has gone awry.

00:10:19.500 --> 00:10:23.120
As outside forces collapsed
inward upon the Mekong,

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:26.300
and most significantly
of which hydro-power,

00:10:26.300 --> 00:10:28.070
and most significantly,
which Chinese hydro-power

00:10:28.070 --> 00:10:29.920
which we'll talk about in a moment.

00:10:29.920 --> 00:10:33.090
This politics of dwelling
becomes more fraught.

00:10:33.090 --> 00:10:35.720
Especially in recent years,
hydro-power projects,

00:10:35.720 --> 00:10:38.580
the expansion of highly
destructive forms of fishing,

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:41.780
fishing with bombs, fishing
with agricultural pesticides,

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:43.203
or fishing with electricity,

00:10:44.690 --> 00:10:46.720
rapid economic and ecological change,

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.590
and an alteration of the
local spirits of place,

00:10:49.590 --> 00:10:52.360
that forever change this
notion of a sense of place

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:54.180
as we have in Ingold, and who's drawing,

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:55.430
of course from Heidegger.

00:10:56.370 --> 00:10:59.050
As I argue here, it's not
an collapse that destroys

00:10:59.050 --> 00:11:02.230
placemaking entirely, but
one which weaves into it

00:11:02.230 --> 00:11:06.863
threads obfuscation and
uncertainty, anxiety, the cult.

00:11:08.330 --> 00:11:10.910
So to zoom out for a moment,
just to situate everybody

00:11:10.910 --> 00:11:12.200
and make sure that we're all talking

00:11:12.200 --> 00:11:13.350
about the same river here.

00:11:13.350 --> 00:11:16.060
The Mekong is one of the largest
in the world by drainage.

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.600
It's the kind of the
aorta of Southeast Asia.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:21.440
It flows down from the Tibetan plateau.

00:11:21.440 --> 00:11:24.363
Through two series of
turbulent Rapids through China.

00:11:25.210 --> 00:11:27.590
Then down forms the border
between Laos and Burma.

00:11:27.590 --> 00:11:29.390
Then between Thailand and Laos.

00:11:29.390 --> 00:11:31.580
Eventually it goes into
the heart of Cambodia,

00:11:31.580 --> 00:11:34.273
and flows out South of Saigon in Vietnam.

00:11:35.160 --> 00:11:37.570
It's a river split, by
political boundaries

00:11:37.570 --> 00:11:39.420
and carries with it a legacy of political

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.820
conflicts and wars.

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.080
But what you're seeing today ...

00:11:44.080 --> 00:11:46.090
Can everybody see the video here?

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:48.020
Is that ...

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:49.801
Can we put ...

00:11:49.801 --> 00:11:51.751
It would be nice (indistinct) video on?

00:11:53.270 --> 00:11:54.580
Oh, yeah, okay.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.823
So this part ...

00:11:56.665 --> 00:11:59.400
(speaks foreign language)

00:11:59.400 --> 00:12:01.730
What you're seeing today, the
river begins to flatten out

00:12:01.730 --> 00:12:03.580
after its turbulent beginnings.

00:12:03.580 --> 00:12:06.290
Stretches are still marked
by Rapids and waterfalls,

00:12:06.290 --> 00:12:08.190
but other regions become broad.

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:11.100
Sometimes this is just a
result of the time of year.

00:12:11.100 --> 00:12:14.200
During the monsoon, the water
level can jump up nine meters

00:12:14.200 --> 00:12:15.940
turning what looks like a scrub forest

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:17.640
into a broad expanse of water,

00:12:17.640 --> 00:12:20.543
and then back down to a
trickle during the dry season.

00:12:22.400 --> 00:12:25.250
Large-scale alteration to the
river has come slowly, right?

00:12:25.250 --> 00:12:27.580
At least in comparison with
many other river systems

00:12:27.580 --> 00:12:28.670
in the world.

00:12:28.670 --> 00:12:32.140
The Mekong region was not
high priority for China,

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:33.220
being far from the centers.

00:12:33.220 --> 00:12:35.090
So power in the middle of Mekong area,

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:37.410
has been war torn for hundreds of years.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:40.680
Well, American audiences might
think of the Vietnam war,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:43.300
the Khymer Rouge, and
these kinds of things,

00:12:43.300 --> 00:12:45.180
even in the 17 and 1800s,

00:12:45.180 --> 00:12:47.880
cities such as the Vientiane
were sacked and burned in wars

00:12:47.880 --> 00:12:51.053
from between Siam and Laos.

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:55.150
But change has come, and
it's come very rapidly now.

00:12:55.150 --> 00:12:57.920
Beijing has resolved to do
something about barriers to trade

00:12:57.920 --> 00:13:00.620
between China and areas
further downstream,

00:13:00.620 --> 00:13:04.190
and as a result they've been
blasting Rapids in the river,

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:07.523
trying to make the flow
navigable by cargo ship.

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.560
These alterations are happening upstream

00:13:10.560 --> 00:13:13.103
of where (indistinct) is right now.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:17.060
Which itself is a little
bit upstream from Vientiane,

00:13:17.060 --> 00:13:19.310
but the changes in the
river are keenly felt.

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:23.940
Specifically, this has
to do with hydro-power.

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.490
The numbers of dams across
the mainstream of the Mekong,

00:13:26.490 --> 00:13:28.390
increases every year.

00:13:28.390 --> 00:13:29.980
Chinese and Lao advocates argue,

00:13:29.980 --> 00:13:32.630
that these dams are necessary
to produce clean power

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:34.170
to fuel national developments.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.840
Although the real
beneficiaries are likely to

00:13:36.840 --> 00:13:40.053
be those businesses involved
in exporting the power.

00:13:41.360 --> 00:13:43.129
There are 11 now in China,
across the main stream

00:13:43.129 --> 00:13:46.300
of the river, before it even
flows into Southeast Asia,

00:13:46.300 --> 00:13:49.090
and especially the Nuozhadu Dam,

00:13:49.090 --> 00:13:51.780
has profound impacts on water downstream.

00:13:51.780 --> 00:13:55.480
Effectively in 2019, completely stopping

00:13:55.480 --> 00:13:56.853
the pulse of the Mekong.

00:13:57.890 --> 00:14:00.760
At other times, the dams release water,

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.180
and leading to unexpected gouts of water

00:14:03.180 --> 00:14:06.553
that rushed downstream causing
chaos to those living below.

00:14:07.950 --> 00:14:11.090
So, in the wake of one
of these floods in 2015,

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.790
I followed one fishermen who
I'm gonna call Bong here.

