00:49:00.060 --> 00:49:02.370
But the time was also so fascinating.

00:49:02.370 --> 00:49:04.870
And what I wanted to ask you about,

00:49:04.870 --> 00:49:06.930
was this idea of the Naga,

00:49:06.930 --> 00:49:10.070
having a certain kind
of temporality, right?

00:49:10.070 --> 00:49:12.100
But at the same time ...

00:49:12.100 --> 00:49:14.240
sorry for the word at the same time.

00:49:14.240 --> 00:49:16.480
But at the same time,

00:49:16.480 --> 00:49:20.960
that people are reflecting on Naga time,

00:49:20.960 --> 00:49:23.457
they're also very easily able to say,

00:49:23.457 --> 00:49:26.990
"Well, there was the
Naga doing x, y, and z

00:49:26.990 --> 00:49:28.920
and now there's not," right?

00:49:28.920 --> 00:49:31.690
So there's a kind of
linearity of before and after,

00:49:31.690 --> 00:49:34.213
that's pretty
straightforward on one level.

00:49:35.060 --> 00:49:38.990
We used to do this, now
we don't do that anymore.

00:49:38.990 --> 00:49:42.540
When the dam came,
everything changed, right?

00:49:42.540 --> 00:49:44.233
And so my question to you,

00:49:45.260 --> 00:49:49.320
is there really like a
kind of metaphysical,

00:49:49.320 --> 00:49:53.020
or you could say ontological,
whatever word is appropriate

00:49:54.160 --> 00:49:57.563
difference about time along the river?

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:02.130
Or is it really just a variation

00:50:02.130 --> 00:50:04.453
of a kind of universal temporality?

00:50:05.358 --> 00:50:06.790
And what are your thoughts on that?

00:50:06.790 --> 00:50:09.570
And maybe we can't really
decide it right now.

00:50:09.570 --> 00:50:11.650
But what are some of the
ways in which you thought

00:50:11.650 --> 00:50:13.590
about that problem?

00:50:13.590 --> 00:50:14.670
And just I'll add to it,

00:50:14.670 --> 00:50:18.083
because even as we see
your friend in there,

00:50:18.980 --> 00:50:22.250
I almost feel a visceral
difference of temporality,

00:50:22.250 --> 00:50:24.970
because I remember my own
times along rivers, right?

00:50:24.970 --> 00:50:26.740
When you're at a river,
you have a different

00:50:26.740 --> 00:50:30.850
kind of engagement with
space time somehow, right?

00:50:30.850 --> 00:50:32.750
And so how do we deal with that,

00:50:32.750 --> 00:50:36.050
layered to the real time
of history, not real-time,

00:50:36.050 --> 00:50:38.000
but that the temporality of history,

00:50:38.000 --> 00:50:40.480
and then the temporality of an alternative

00:50:40.480 --> 00:50:42.063
temporality along a river?

00:50:43.370 --> 00:50:44.900
- I mean time is a perception, right?

00:50:44.900 --> 00:50:47.180
So time depends upon your apprehension

00:50:47.180 --> 00:50:49.680
of time and your experience of time,

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:51.360
and yeah, multiple different registers.

00:50:51.360 --> 00:50:53.970
And I think one, I start off the book,

00:50:53.970 --> 00:50:58.690
and talking about the
messianic and apocalyptic

00:50:58.690 --> 00:50:59.740
time experiences.

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:02.320
So, when people talked about the dams,

00:51:02.320 --> 00:51:04.990
a lot of times I start
off with a dream that

00:51:07.336 --> 00:51:09.940
what one of my interlocutors
had, about the dam breaking,

00:51:09.940 --> 00:51:14.580
and he dreamt that he
saw a gap in the dam,

00:51:14.580 --> 00:51:17.030
and it was gonna one day crack in half,

00:51:17.030 --> 00:51:19.230
and flood and destroy the entire town.

00:51:19.230 --> 00:51:22.370
And so, that also that
places a particular marker

00:51:22.370 --> 00:51:26.010
in the future of what happens
when these things fail,

00:51:26.010 --> 00:51:28.430
or what happens when
they succeed too much,

00:51:28.430 --> 00:51:30.430
what happens when we can no longer wait?

00:51:31.610 --> 00:51:34.150
So there's this future
points, and then of course,

00:51:34.150 --> 00:51:36.720
as you were saying there's
this sort of a past point too,

00:51:36.720 --> 00:51:40.060
that like before the dam
came, it was like this.

00:51:40.060 --> 00:51:42.030
And there's also, I think this feeds into,

00:51:42.030 --> 00:51:43.330
there's a number of different registers

00:51:43.330 --> 00:51:44.470
that this feeds into.

00:51:44.470 --> 00:51:46.440
One is the cult of developments,

00:51:46.440 --> 00:51:50.410
that is endemic many,
many places in the world,

00:51:50.410 --> 00:51:51.530
maybe every place in the world.

00:51:51.530 --> 00:51:55.460
But this idea that like that
things can continually progress

00:51:55.460 --> 00:51:58.220
along a certain kind of
a linear path towards

00:51:58.220 --> 00:52:01.170
more complexity, or more
developments, or we get ...

00:52:01.170 --> 00:52:02.800
I remember the year
when we got electricity.

00:52:02.800 --> 00:52:06.070
I remember the year when the,
where the road got paved.

00:52:06.070 --> 00:52:07.200
I remember the year ...

00:52:07.200 --> 00:52:08.920
These kinds of markers there.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:11.480
And so that projects in the future,

00:52:11.480 --> 00:52:13.270
kind of an upward trajectory,

00:52:13.270 --> 00:52:18.060
that can then be cast
into doubt by intimations,

00:52:18.060 --> 00:52:20.010
that, hey, there might
be disaster actually

00:52:20.010 --> 00:52:20.920
at the end of this.

00:52:20.920 --> 00:52:23.020
And so there are these points,

00:52:23.020 --> 00:52:26.750
and various points in history,
and especially in Isan,

00:52:26.750 --> 00:52:30.070
where you get this notion
of messianic time comes in.

00:52:30.070 --> 00:52:31.043
There's a ...

00:52:32.190 --> 00:52:33.023
What is it?

00:52:34.770 --> 00:52:36.970
Millenarian group.

00:52:36.970 --> 00:52:38.750
Not the millennials, there's something ...

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:41.170
There also apocalyptic but
there's something else.

00:52:41.170 --> 00:52:43.690
But there's a movement in
the early two thousands,

00:52:43.690 --> 00:52:45.960
or the early 19, fairly 20th century,

00:52:45.960 --> 00:52:48.450
that was positing there would come a time

00:52:48.450 --> 00:52:50.840
when everything would change its shape.

00:52:50.840 --> 00:52:53.430
So, animals would become demons.

00:52:53.430 --> 00:52:55.910
Money would become excrement.

00:52:55.910 --> 00:52:58.880
And the only thing solid in
the world would be lemon grass.

00:52:58.880 --> 00:53:00.410
Since he would've had
to hold onto the stalks

00:53:00.410 --> 00:53:02.430
of lemon grass, don't
keep from being blown away

00:53:02.430 --> 00:53:05.710
by a giant wind that would
sweep through the land.

00:53:05.710 --> 00:53:08.670
Also, there was the communist movement,

00:53:08.670 --> 00:53:12.820
which was tremendously
based in the province,

00:53:12.820 --> 00:53:16.030
just very, very close to
this area in which people

00:53:16.030 --> 00:53:18.570
in this village, and others
up and down in Mekong had

00:53:18.570 --> 00:53:20.979
complicated relationships with.

00:53:20.979 --> 00:53:23.260
So, and of course,
communism they also posited

00:53:23.260 --> 00:53:27.950
a time free of imperialism,
a time of revolution,

00:53:27.950 --> 00:53:29.550
the constant revolution.

00:53:29.550 --> 00:53:32.790
So this constant deferment
of disaster and hope

00:53:32.790 --> 00:53:34.550
often the future marks another point,

00:53:34.550 --> 00:53:37.370
at which there's a future
point, there's a past point,

00:53:37.370 --> 00:53:41.680
at which things were mythical, mystical,

00:53:41.680 --> 00:53:43.130
when Naga was still alive.

00:53:43.130 --> 00:53:46.010
And there you are caught somehow
between these two points,

00:53:46.010 --> 00:53:49.220
and so you're waiting
for something to happen

00:53:49.220 --> 00:53:51.810
in between these two, the
future point and the past point

00:53:51.810 --> 00:53:52.643
you're caught.

00:53:55.750 --> 00:53:58.330
And I think, yeah, there is a certain ...

00:53:58.330 --> 00:54:01.683
About the actual, (indistinct)

00:54:03.480 --> 00:54:05.150
It's a bit to talk about the time,

00:54:05.150 --> 00:54:06.950
as that's the materiality of it,

00:54:06.950 --> 00:54:10.100
the time that's embedded within
the material of the river.

00:54:10.100 --> 00:54:12.310
That also has its own particular rhythms,

00:54:12.310 --> 00:54:16.360
and its own particular cycles beyond

00:54:16.360 --> 00:54:18.323
just the pulse of the Mekong.

00:54:19.650 --> 00:54:22.870
But the way that time
passes in a particular day,

00:54:22.870 --> 00:54:24.710
there's boats that come at night,

00:54:24.710 --> 00:54:26.440
which are the fish shockers,

00:54:26.440 --> 00:54:29.510
or maybe people running
meth from across the border

00:54:29.510 --> 00:54:31.130
which happens.

00:54:31.130 --> 00:54:33.510
Something untoward happening
in the middle of the night,

00:54:33.510 --> 00:54:37.390
or maybe it's just your friends
coming back from illicit pub

00:54:37.390 --> 00:54:40.030
in Laos really late.

00:54:40.030 --> 00:54:42.580
Then the chanting starts
up around four or five

00:54:42.580 --> 00:54:45.530
in the morning, and
then the animals start,

00:54:45.530 --> 00:54:47.990
and then the traffic starts,
and the morning boats start,

00:54:47.990 --> 00:54:52.060
and so there's a whole
auditory sensory perception

00:54:52.060 --> 00:54:54.750
that informs you in a
very bodily way about

00:54:54.750 --> 00:54:55.730
the passing of the time,

00:54:55.730 --> 00:54:57.330
that is always connected to the river.

00:54:57.330 --> 00:55:01.160
That's always linked to
that feeling of listening

00:55:01.160 --> 00:55:02.370
to what the river is doing,

00:55:02.370 --> 00:55:04.260
what people are doing on top of the river,

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:06.620
informs you of your sense of time,

00:55:06.620 --> 00:55:08.570
of your sense of the passage of time.

00:55:08.570 --> 00:55:10.550
So between like, yeah, the metaphysical,

00:55:10.550 --> 00:55:15.550
the apocalyptic messianic
potentials in the future.

00:55:15.770 --> 00:55:18.310
and then the kind of
experience of everyday

00:55:18.310 --> 00:55:20.453
passage of time too.

00:55:20.453 --> 00:55:22.630
I found time temporality and time,

00:55:22.630 --> 00:55:25.430
and those debates (indistinct),
and these debates about,

00:55:25.430 --> 00:55:27.750
we should really be saying
time and not temporality.

00:55:27.750 --> 00:55:32.450
But this notion of the
affective passage of time

00:55:32.450 --> 00:55:34.490
is something that I really ...

00:55:34.490 --> 00:55:37.053
it's always very striking, I think.

00:55:38.870 --> 00:55:40.090
- Thank you.

00:55:40.090 --> 00:55:41.900
We have one question from Al.

00:55:41.900 --> 00:55:43.160
Go ahead Al.

00:55:43.160 --> 00:55:45.778
- Yeah, so kind of related to this,

00:55:45.778 --> 00:55:48.100
something I took away from
the book was this idea

00:55:48.100 --> 00:55:51.390
of ambivalence or productive
potential of maybe.

00:55:51.390 --> 00:55:53.810
So you talk in the
opening (indistinct) about

00:55:53.810 --> 00:55:55.500
how there's a sense of ambivalence.

00:55:55.500 --> 00:55:57.470
And that, to me was really interesting.

00:55:57.470 --> 00:55:59.870
Just taking seriously that ambivalence

00:55:59.870 --> 00:56:02.980
with what we call a hunter
logical alternative,

00:56:02.980 --> 00:56:04.270
so I thought that was super interesting.

00:56:04.270 --> 00:56:07.540
But, I'm wondering how you reconcile that,

00:56:07.540 --> 00:56:10.710
with a kind of anthropology
that's emerging

00:56:10.710 --> 00:56:12.690
in terms of practices,
like people who want to go

00:56:12.690 --> 00:56:16.170
with complicit, and like who
are the stakeholders at work,

00:56:16.170 --> 00:56:19.120
and politics and accountability,

00:56:19.120 --> 00:56:21.550
so how do you juggle with the kind of

00:56:21.550 --> 00:56:24.800
ambivalence and accountability?

00:56:24.800 --> 00:56:27.260
One is asking for kind
of like taking seriously

00:56:27.260 --> 00:56:28.093
that murkiness.

00:56:28.093 --> 00:56:29.800
But the other is murky,

00:56:29.800 --> 00:56:31.505
but people wanna shine a light on it.

00:56:31.505 --> 00:56:36.460
How do you position yourself
between these holes in a sense?

00:56:36.460 --> 00:56:37.293
- That's a good question.

00:56:37.293 --> 00:56:41.120
And that's also something
that I think about a lot.

00:56:41.120 --> 00:56:43.580
And, I'd like to say I do both, right?

00:56:43.580 --> 00:56:45.110
And that that's like the right answer.

00:56:45.110 --> 00:56:49.080
But, so explain why I do both.

00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:52.960
I mean, first off, I
have some very practical

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:57.443
and pragmatic alliances, that I think,

00:56:58.960 --> 00:57:01.500
in the town where I work in that region,

00:57:01.500 --> 00:57:06.490
there's a number of
NGOs that I meet up with

00:57:06.490 --> 00:57:09.400
when I'm down there, and very practical.

00:57:09.400 --> 00:57:10.830
Making sure that what I do,

00:57:10.830 --> 00:57:15.180
doesn't damage the site
where I work, and in fact,

00:57:15.180 --> 00:57:20.180
I'm able to help along the
interests of the people

00:57:21.110 --> 00:57:22.940
with whom I work as much as I can.

00:57:22.940 --> 00:57:24.550
On the other hand too,

00:57:24.550 --> 00:57:27.690
I mean, I'm personally coming
from a very deconstructive

00:57:27.690 --> 00:57:30.190
kind of anthropology, where
I think it's in reflexive.

00:57:30.190 --> 00:57:33.420
I think it's always vital to
question your own assumptions

00:57:33.420 --> 00:57:37.290
when you're coming into
a field work situation.

00:57:37.290 --> 00:57:38.997
So questioning the
stakeholders, and saying,

00:57:38.997 --> 00:57:43.997
"Okay, can we really chart out
the ethically right position,

00:57:46.140 --> 00:57:47.830
the politically right position to take,

00:57:47.830 --> 00:57:50.200
and then identify the
stakeholders involved.

00:57:50.200 --> 00:57:53.650
I think Marisol Dilla Kadana
talks a little bit about this.

00:57:53.650 --> 00:57:56.220
And incorporating non human stakeholders

00:57:56.220 --> 00:58:01.220
within the thing, within your
plurry verse as she says,

00:58:01.730 --> 00:58:03.523
within you're a political unit.

00:58:05.510 --> 00:58:07.030
That's good.

00:58:07.030 --> 00:58:10.460
I think that speaks towards
taking other ways of seeing

00:58:10.460 --> 00:58:14.580
the world seriously,
but in addition to that,

00:58:14.580 --> 00:58:17.780
I think we also need to recognize ...

00:58:17.780 --> 00:58:19.000
And this is really important to me.

00:58:19.000 --> 00:58:21.400
We need to see the other than human,

00:58:21.400 --> 00:58:25.790
the non-human as our
interlocutors see it, or as ...

00:58:25.790 --> 00:58:29.740
And not as like their
culture or their ontology

00:58:29.740 --> 00:58:30.573
or something like that.

00:58:30.573 --> 00:58:32.360
But we are all in a way questioning.

00:58:32.360 --> 00:58:35.940
And myself included, questioning
what exists in the world.

00:58:35.940 --> 00:58:38.360
And having real debates about
what exists in the world.

00:58:38.360 --> 00:58:43.360
I remember having a talk
with another friend of mine

00:58:43.380 --> 00:58:46.320
from the same region.

00:58:46.320 --> 00:58:49.620
And friend of mine who we had been friends

00:58:49.620 --> 00:58:53.313
since 2014 or something
like this, and he asked me,

00:58:55.656 --> 00:58:56.900
"Do you believe in ghosts?"

00:58:56.900 --> 00:58:59.770
And this is something that I get a lot,

00:58:59.770 --> 00:59:00.840
I write a lot about ghosts,

00:59:00.840 --> 00:59:02.200
and I'm very used to this question.

00:59:02.200 --> 00:59:04.380
And so I answered, (speaking
on foreign language).

00:59:09.660 --> 00:59:11.570
Which is the standard thing
you're supposed to say,

00:59:11.570 --> 00:59:13.680
and he got angry with me.

00:59:13.680 --> 00:59:15.890
And he said, "Look, I
asked you a question,

00:59:15.890 --> 00:59:17.737
and I'm asking you sincerely."

00:59:19.820 --> 00:59:22.937
And I realized that, I'd been
using that as a way of saying,

00:59:22.937 --> 00:59:26.480
"Okay, so this is what I'm
interested in what you believe,

00:59:26.480 --> 00:59:31.480
and your ideas, and now I'm
standing on my other side,

00:59:31.820 --> 00:59:32.880
of my anthropologist side.

00:59:32.880 --> 00:59:34.410
I'm gonna write this down, I'm gonna say,

00:59:34.410 --> 00:59:36.193
okay, so this is Thai.

00:59:37.790 --> 00:59:40.030
Metaphysical reality, ontological reality.

00:59:40.030 --> 00:59:40.930
What are you gonna say?"

00:59:40.930 --> 00:59:42.970
No, I wasn't being taken as that.

00:59:42.970 --> 00:59:45.620
I was being taken as a
co-investigator in the world.

00:59:45.620 --> 00:59:46.927
And so what he said is, he says,

00:59:46.927 --> 00:59:48.713
"You've been around many places.

00:59:49.784 --> 00:59:53.200
You studied a lot, you
must know something.

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:54.610
So, what do you know?

00:59:54.610 --> 00:59:56.290
And let's compare."

00:59:56.290 --> 00:59:59.950
And, I realized that he's
asking you to be taken seriously

00:59:59.950 --> 01:00:02.330
as a co-investigator of a world.

01:00:02.330 --> 01:00:03.820
And that, I think as an anthropologist

01:00:03.820 --> 01:00:06.100
is very important for us to do that,

01:00:06.100 --> 01:00:11.100
to recognize that our
interlocutors are collaborators

01:00:12.915 --> 01:00:14.240
and not just in the sense of like,

01:00:14.240 --> 01:00:15.840
Oh, we're gonna get together,

01:00:15.840 --> 01:00:20.073
and maybe try to give photo
credit, or co-author something,

01:00:21.148 --> 01:00:22.660
or co-present something.

01:00:22.660 --> 01:00:26.110
But co-investigators into
the kinds of questions

01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:26.943
we're asking.

01:00:26.943 --> 01:00:28.450
Nobody has a distinct answer.

01:00:28.450 --> 01:00:30.120
So who are the stakeholders?

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:32.330
Is the Naga stakeholder?

01:00:32.330 --> 01:00:34.853
I don't know, nobody knows.

01:00:36.373 --> 01:00:38.040
And I think answering that honestly

01:00:38.040 --> 01:00:43.040
is one way of getting
true to the heart of what

01:00:43.390 --> 01:00:46.260
the ethnography means to
destabilize your own sense

01:00:46.260 --> 01:00:49.050
of shorty about what exists
and how the world works.

01:00:49.050 --> 01:00:51.670
Which is what I think the
point of anthropology is.

01:00:51.670 --> 01:00:52.963
So, let's stop there.

01:00:56.240 --> 01:00:57.073
Co-researcher?

01:00:57.073 --> 01:00:58.160
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:00:58.160 --> 01:00:58.993
That's ...

01:00:58.993 --> 01:01:02.580
- Paul, before I let
you ask your question,

01:01:02.580 --> 01:01:05.090
I wanted to just say one little thing.

01:01:05.090 --> 01:01:07.430
I wrote that word co-researcher because

01:01:08.710 --> 01:01:13.710
when you were speaking about
co-investigator, Andrew,

01:01:14.500 --> 01:01:18.610
it reminded me of a misreading
that may be actually

01:01:18.610 --> 01:01:20.020
interesting.

01:01:20.020 --> 01:01:22.570
'Cause when I read your
book, you used the term,

01:01:22.570 --> 01:01:24.570
and I think in the introduction somewhere,

01:01:24.570 --> 01:01:25.930
I can't remember what page.

01:01:25.930 --> 01:01:27.820
But the word co-researcher,

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:29.860
which I just wrote in
the chat for everyone.

01:01:29.860 --> 01:01:32.790
But when I first read the word,

01:01:32.790 --> 01:01:35.080
and this is honest truth,

01:01:35.080 --> 01:01:38.450
I thought I'd never seen that
word written like that before,

01:01:38.450 --> 01:01:41.790
and I thought that it meant core searcher.

01:01:41.790 --> 01:01:42.910
- That's ...

01:01:42.910 --> 01:01:43.780
Yeah.

01:01:43.780 --> 01:01:45.767
- And the story that you're
telling, and then I said,

01:01:45.767 --> 01:01:47.860
"Oh, actually he means co-researcher."

01:01:47.860 --> 01:01:49.700
But then I remember pausing thinking,

01:01:49.700 --> 01:01:51.790
well, actually what he's saying,

01:01:51.790 --> 01:01:53.630
is that we're all in this together,

01:01:53.630 --> 01:01:55.590
as searching for the core, right?

01:01:55.590 --> 01:01:58.100
Which is sort of what the
story you just described

01:01:58.100 --> 01:01:59.710
with your friend there.

01:01:59.710 --> 01:02:03.810
He's like, "No, we're
not just co-investigator,

01:02:03.810 --> 01:02:06.950
we're core searchers, and
we're doing it together,

01:02:06.950 --> 01:02:09.210
looking for the core of human existence

01:02:09.210 --> 01:02:11.070
or something like that, or something."

01:02:11.070 --> 01:02:13.420
And so, I just wanted
to do that play on words

01:02:13.420 --> 01:02:14.450
it was unintentional.

01:02:14.450 --> 01:02:16.350
But I know that Paul has a question.

01:02:16.350 --> 01:02:18.850
But you can respond to
that real quick if you want

01:02:24.990 --> 01:02:28.070
- Paul and then I'll get some
Williams questions, yeah.

01:02:28.070 --> 01:02:31.680
- I was curious, I had
the feeling that perhaps

01:02:31.680 --> 01:02:35.360
these fishermen and
farmers on the Thai side,

01:02:35.360 --> 01:02:38.020
must be complaining at
least to the regional

01:02:38.020 --> 01:02:41.320
Thai government if not
the central government,

01:02:41.320 --> 01:02:43.240
about what's happening.

01:02:43.240 --> 01:02:44.980
What is the central government,

01:02:44.980 --> 01:02:48.070
or the regional government say to them?

01:02:48.070 --> 01:02:49.510
And I had the same question.

01:02:49.510 --> 01:02:51.860
I don't know if you know what the Lao

01:02:51.860 --> 01:02:53.933
fishermen and farmers are saying?

01:02:57.550 --> 01:02:59.520
- My answer to that question
would have been different

01:02:59.520 --> 01:03:00.700
two years ago.

01:03:00.700 --> 01:03:05.550
And I think, it's a side anecdote.

01:03:05.550 --> 01:03:09.410
There was one possession ritual,

01:03:09.410 --> 01:03:14.410
sort of a spirit medium
ritual that I was involved in.

01:03:15.050 --> 01:03:17.130
Where the people were
asking the spirit medium

01:03:17.130 --> 01:03:18.760
to kind of stop the dams,

01:03:18.760 --> 01:03:20.390
and get the spirits to stop the dams.

01:03:20.390 --> 01:03:24.453
And the spirit calls up the
Laotian spirit on the phone,

01:03:26.930 --> 01:03:30.030
and to ask, to request a stop to the dam.

01:03:30.030 --> 01:03:31.757
And that what the Laotian spirit says is,

01:03:31.757 --> 01:03:34.370
"This is what is necessary
for national developments."

01:03:34.370 --> 01:03:36.020
And I think that would be the answer

01:03:36.020 --> 01:03:38.180
that you would get from
the Lao government,

01:03:38.180 --> 01:03:41.590
as well as the Lao spirit, the Lord too.

01:03:41.590 --> 01:03:44.220
And I think, that's no accident
that the two were speaking

01:03:44.220 --> 01:03:45.440
in the same voice.

01:03:45.440 --> 01:03:50.440
On the Thai side, now
there's a lot of electricity.

01:03:50.440 --> 01:03:55.090
In Bangkok, I'm sure many of
you have spent time there.

01:03:55.090 --> 01:03:57.860
You have shopping malls
that consume as much,

01:03:57.860 --> 01:04:01.640
individual shopping malls, that
consume as much electricity

01:04:01.640 --> 01:04:03.490
as an entire province.

01:04:03.490 --> 01:04:04.940
The province of (indistinct),

01:04:06.540 --> 01:04:09.720
uses half as much electricity
as the shopping mall

01:04:09.720 --> 01:04:10.933
of Siam Paragon.

01:04:12.800 --> 01:04:17.150
So, Thailand has always been
a center-oriented state.

01:04:17.150 --> 01:04:19.520
Where resources are pulled to the center.

01:04:19.520 --> 01:04:22.160
They're part of the
pushback against the regime

01:04:22.160 --> 01:04:25.060
of Thaksin Shinawatra
was a pushback against

01:04:25.060 --> 01:04:27.160
the sort of distribution of wealth

01:04:27.160 --> 01:04:28.610
a little bit outside of the center,

01:04:28.610 --> 01:04:30.780
even though it was going
to his own projects.

01:04:30.780 --> 01:04:35.693
But, the drawing in of
resources to the center,

01:04:37.120 --> 01:04:40.780
has meant that the lives of
of people on the outskirts

01:04:40.780 --> 01:04:41.960
don't get much play.

01:04:41.960 --> 01:04:44.990
However, recently, this year even I think,

01:04:44.990 --> 01:04:48.380
Thailand has formally complained
to the Lao government,

01:04:48.380 --> 01:04:50.860
and has intimated that it
will not purchase electricity

01:04:50.860 --> 01:04:52.785
from some of the new dams.

01:04:52.785 --> 01:04:55.970
(indistinct) it's purchasing from.

01:04:55.970 --> 01:04:57.423
But there are some of the new dams there,

01:04:57.423 --> 01:04:59.660
that Thai government has
said it's not gonna to.

01:04:59.660 --> 01:05:02.000
I don't know why, I don't
know what's behind that.

01:05:02.000 --> 01:05:04.180
It may be good intentions.

01:05:04.180 --> 01:05:05.970
It may be honorable intentions.

01:05:05.970 --> 01:05:09.573
I don't trust it, but maybe.

01:05:11.010 --> 01:05:11.910
But there is an act-

01:05:11.910 --> 01:05:13.080
- [Paul] Is most-

01:05:13.080 --> 01:05:13.930
- I'm sorry.

01:05:13.930 --> 01:05:16.350
- Is most of the electricity being sold to

01:05:16.350 --> 01:05:17.813
Thailand or to China?

01:05:18.660 --> 01:05:19.493
- To Thai.

01:05:19.493 --> 01:05:23.500
The electricity generated in
Laos, is going to Thailand.

01:05:23.500 --> 01:05:25.770
Further upstream, some
of that may go to China.

01:05:25.770 --> 01:05:28.120
But even the Chinese
names right on the border,

01:05:29.520 --> 01:05:31.020
that's being sold for capital.

01:05:32.770 --> 01:05:36.810
So William asks, speaking of co-research,

01:05:36.810 --> 01:05:38.530
are you engaged in or
thinking of engaging in

01:05:38.530 --> 01:05:39.870
multi-sided and extended project,

01:05:39.870 --> 01:05:41.610
that incorporates other
anthropologists working

01:05:41.610 --> 01:05:44.360
both up and downstream in
China, Cambodia, and Vietnam?

01:05:45.630 --> 01:05:49.263
William, are you in charge of
a large grant making projects?

01:05:51.240 --> 01:05:53.740
We could answer our own questions here.

01:05:53.740 --> 01:05:54.940
Not yet, sad face.

01:05:54.940 --> 01:05:58.770
Okay, well, let me know
when you are and we'll talk.

01:05:58.770 --> 01:06:00.573
But I think practically speaking,

01:06:01.800 --> 01:06:02.960
I would actually love to get involved

01:06:02.960 --> 01:06:06.440
with our fishery scientists too.

01:06:06.440 --> 01:06:09.600
Because the actual ...

01:06:09.600 --> 01:06:12.460
There has not been a lot
of good fisheries studies

01:06:14.819 --> 01:06:15.720
on the Mekong river.

01:06:15.720 --> 01:06:18.080
Further down in Cambodia,
there have been some.

01:06:18.080 --> 01:06:19.750
But the middle Mekong region,

01:06:19.750 --> 01:06:23.810
and especially as you go
further up into Laos and China,

01:06:23.810 --> 01:06:28.203
the barriers doing environmental
field work are increased.

01:06:29.350 --> 01:06:32.373
Laos famously, some years ago,

01:06:33.630 --> 01:06:37.553
environmental activists
disappeared, and presumably ...

01:06:40.440 --> 01:06:42.420
Make it interdisciplinary
and add a geographer.

01:06:42.420 --> 01:06:44.380
Sure, yes.

01:06:44.380 --> 01:06:46.333
Now let's find grants everyone.

01:06:50.270 --> 01:06:54.480
- I think you've seen with
our modest evening right here

01:06:54.480 --> 01:06:58.550
we've created a Mekong studies coalition.

01:06:58.550 --> 01:07:01.770
I mean, so I hope all
of you can be in touch

01:07:01.770 --> 01:07:02.603
with each other.

01:07:02.603 --> 01:07:05.403
But it's quite clear that
the work is important.

01:07:06.860 --> 01:07:09.560
I wanna bring it to a close right now,

01:07:09.560 --> 01:07:12.120
with our evening discussions.

01:07:12.120 --> 01:07:14.340
I try not to go too late just to make sure

01:07:14.340 --> 01:07:16.760
that we all don't get exhausted.

01:07:16.760 --> 01:07:19.330
But if anyone has a final question

01:07:19.330 --> 01:07:22.420
they're really dying to ask,
we can do that right now.

01:07:22.420 --> 01:07:25.000
Otherwise, we'll turn off the recorder

01:07:27.407 --> 01:07:28.890
and bring the session to a close.

01:07:28.890 --> 01:07:33.113
So, let me just see if
there's any further questions.

01:07:36.560 --> 01:07:40.550
And I do hope that folks will, sorry,

01:07:40.550 --> 01:07:44.490
tune to some of the
upcoming events webinar

01:07:44.490 --> 01:07:47.266
Southeast Asia Council Brown Bag series,

01:07:47.266 --> 01:07:52.240
and in our side Eddy
series on waters here.

01:07:52.240 --> 01:07:54.830
I will draw your attention
in particular to our

01:07:54.830 --> 01:07:56.280
talk in the Brown Bag.

01:07:56.280 --> 01:07:59.370
Next week, we'll be Vince Raphael,

01:07:59.370 --> 01:08:02.480
who will be giving a a very
provocatively titled talk

01:08:03.436 --> 01:08:04.320
called Duterte phallus.

01:08:04.320 --> 01:08:06.100
I don't have the full subtitle.

01:08:06.100 --> 01:08:10.430
But it's the aesthetics of
vulgarity in Duterte Philippines.

01:08:10.430 --> 01:08:13.000
So I think it will be
quite a great experience,

01:08:13.000 --> 01:08:14.980
and I hope some of you
can join us for that.

01:08:14.980 --> 01:08:17.800
Those talks are on Wednesdays at noon.

01:08:17.800 --> 01:08:21.100
And then there's a few other
series, in the water series.

01:08:21.100 --> 01:08:23.810
And then at the end of
the academic semester,

01:08:23.810 --> 01:08:26.250
there's the big graduate
student conference.

01:08:26.250 --> 01:08:28.910
And, so I hope we can all tune into some

01:08:28.910 --> 01:08:30.728
of these other events.

01:08:30.728 --> 01:08:32.133
Thanks everyone.

